{"aggregate":{"crossover_predicted_mass_fraction_25C":0.037171,"crossover_prediction_bracketed_at_all_T":true,"finding":"Composing Raoult (1887), Dalton (1802) and the OSHA additive mixture rule reduces EXACTLY to a mole-fraction-weighted sum of the module's existing pure-component VHRs, which supplies the blend-level index the single-substance receipts lacked. Two operational consequences follow. (i) Thinner specs are written in mass fractions; using them directly as weights mis-states the blend hazard index because the panel's molar masses span 3.3x. (ii) The dominant contributor is set by mole fraction times VHR, not by VHR alone, so a trace fast solvent has a closed-form dominance-crossover mass fraction above which it governs the ventilation requirement -- for methanol carried over into an aromatic thinner that crossover sits at a few weight percent.","h0_mass_surrogate":{"adequacy_criterion_relative_error":0.05,"hypothesis":"H0_MASS_SURROGATE: mass-fraction weighting of pure-component VHRs is an adequate surrogate for the mole-fraction-correct Raoult blend index.","interpretation":"Refuted. Reading a thinner spec's mass fractions as if they were mole fractions mis-states, with a blend-dependent SIGN, the saturated-headspace mixture hazard index by up to 39.34% across the 15-40 C envelope. Mechanism: the panel's molar masses span 32.04-106.17 g/mol (3.31x), so on conversion to mole fractions the LIGHTER constituents are up-weighted and the heavier ones down-weighted. The sign of the resulting error therefore depends on whether a blend's light constituent is more hazardous than its heavy one: in methanol- and MEK-bearing blends the light constituent carries the higher VHR and mass weighting UNDER-states the index (worst case -37.63%), whereas in the xylene/n-butanol thinner the lighter alcohol is the LESS hazardous constituent and mass weighting OVER-states it (1.74%). A single directional correction factor is therefore not available; the mole-fraction conversion has to be done.","max_abs_relative_error_over_blends_and_T":0.393417,"sign_of_error_all_negative":false,"sign_of_error_all_positive":false,"verdict":"REFUTED","worst_blend":"anti_blush_alcohol_rich"},"h0_pure_worst_actor":{"blends_where_violated":["aromatic_100_surrogate","wash_carryover_methanol_1pct","wash_carryover_methanol_3pct"],"hypothesis":"H0_PURE_WORST_ACTOR: the constituent with the highest pure-component VHR is the dominant contributor to the blend's inhalation hazard.","interpretation":"Refuted on at least one realistic blend: the pure-component worst actor is NOT always the dominant blend contributor, because contribution scales with mole fraction. A trace high-volatility solvent can be the single worst substance on the shelf and still be a minor contributor below its dominance-crossover mass fraction -- and can take over above it. Single-substance VHR prescreening therefore mis-assigns the ventilation-priority target for blends near the crossover.","n_blends":7,"n_holds_at_25C":4,"verdict":"REFUTED"},"identity_exact":true,"max_identity_abs_error":5.684341886080802e-14,"min_gamma_crit_at_25C":1.2485,"n_blends_mass_surrogate_preserves_dominant_at_25C":6,"n_crossover_pairs":8,"n_crossovers_below_5wt_pct":1},"binary_dominance_crossovers":[{"bulk_solvent":"m_xylene","crossover_mass_fraction_15C":0.464489,"crossover_mass_fraction_25C":0.466014,"crossover_mass_fraction_40C":0.468244,"crossover_shift_15_to_40C":0.003755,"trace_dominates_below_5wt_pct":false,"trace_solvent":"ethylbenzene"},{"bulk_solvent":"m_xylene","crossover_mass_fraction_15C":0.100772,"crossover_mass_fraction_25C":0.1111,"crossover_mass_fraction_40C":0.12651,"crossover_shift_15_to_40C":0.025738,"trace_dominates_below_5wt_pct":false,"trace_solvent":"mek_2_butanone"},{"bulk_solvent":"m_xylene","crossover_mass_fraction_15C":0.035486,"crossover_mass_fraction_25C":0.037929,"crossover_mass_fraction_40C":0.041217,"crossover_shift_15_to_40C":0.005731,"trace_dominates_below_5wt_pct":true,"trace_sol